<Project>

    <!--
      Prerelease packages must be accepted deliberately, not inherited.

      NuGetAudit already fails the build on a KNOWN VULNERABILITY. Nothing warns about a package that is
      simply not finished. That gap is not theoretical here: `OpenTelemetry.Exporter.Prometheus.AspNetCore`
      was pinned at a beta and stayed pinned, and it took a deliberate check of the NuGet feed on 2026-08-06
      to discover it has been in prerelease for FOUR YEARS — 33 versions, not one stable, since 2022-08-18 —
      while every other package in the same OpenTelemetry suite shipped stable 1.17.0 on the same day.

      A pin nobody re-examines becomes a decision nobody made. So: every prerelease version in
      Directory.Packages.props raises OVERFITPRERELEASE at build time, unless it is listed below with a
      reason and a date. Accepting one is then an explicit, reviewable act rather than silence.

      Warning, never error, and on purpose. A prerelease dependency is a judgement call about support and
      risk, not a rule violation — promoting it to an error would only teach people to add entries to the
      accept-list without reading them.

      Emitted once, from Main, rather than once per project: twenty-five copies of the same warning is how a
      warning becomes wallpaper.

      Turn it off for one build with -p:OverfitPrereleaseCheck=false. If this file itself misbehaves, deleting
      it restores the previous behaviour exactly — it adds nothing else.
    -->

    <ItemGroup>
        <!--
          Accepted prereleases. One entry per package, each with WHY and WHEN, so the next reader can judge
          whether the reason still holds instead of assuming somebody thought about it.

          Nothing is accepted, and as of 2026-08-11 there is nothing to accept: the tree has NO prerelease
          pin at all. `OpenTelemetry.Exporter.Prometheus.AspNetCore` was the one, was deliberately never
          listed here, and is now gone — Demo/LocalAgentAspNetDemo writes its own Prometheus exposition
          (Observability/BucketHistogram.cs + MetricsCollector.WriteExposition), which is what the product
          already does in Sources/Server.AspNet and Sources/Anomalies. XC-6 in docs/TASKS.md carries the
          reasoning, including why hand-rolling beat the stable OTLP exporter here: the demo's compose stack
          SCRAPES /metrics, so OTLP would have needed a collector added to it.

          That is this check working as intended, and the only evidence it has: a pin nobody re-examined for
          four years became visible, then became a decision, then became a removal. Keep the list empty if
          you can — an entry here is a standing exception, not a fix.
        -->
        <!-- <OverfitAcceptedPrerelease Include="Some.Package" Reason="why" Since="yyyy-mm-dd" /> -->
    </ItemGroup>

    <Target Name="OverfitPrereleaseCheck"
            BeforeTargets="CoreCompile"
            Condition="'$(OverfitPrereleaseCheck)' != 'false' AND '$(MSBuildProjectName)' == 'Main'">

        <ItemGroup>
            <_OverfitPrerelease Include="@(PackageVersion)"
                                Condition="$([System.String]::Copy('%(PackageVersion.Version)').Contains('-'))" />
            <_OverfitPrereleaseUnaccepted Include="@(_OverfitPrerelease)"
                                          Exclude="@(OverfitAcceptedPrerelease)" />
        </ItemGroup>

        <!--
          The Condition is load-bearing, not defensive. Measured 2026-08-07: with the only prerelease
          package added to the accept-list above, `_OverfitPrereleaseUnaccepted` is correctly empty — and
          the Warning task STILL ran once, batching over the empty list and producing

              " is pinned at a PRERELEASE version () and is not on the accepted list."

          with no package name and no version. That is worse than having no accept-list at all: accepting
          a package deliberately left the build shouting about nothing in particular. An empty item list
          must skip the task, and only an explicit emptiness check makes it do so.
        -->
        <Warning Condition="'@(_OverfitPrereleaseUnaccepted)' != ''"
                 Code="OVERFITPRERELEASE"
                 File="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)Directory.Packages.props"
                 Text="%(_OverfitPrereleaseUnaccepted.Identity) is pinned at a PRERELEASE version (%(_OverfitPrereleaseUnaccepted.Version)) and is not on the accepted list. Check whether a stable release exists before keeping it: a package can sit in prerelease for years while the rest of its suite ships stable. Accept it deliberately by adding an OverfitAcceptedPrerelease entry with a reason and a date in Directory.Build.targets, or move off it." />

    </Target>

    <!--
      A build is the third consumer of this machine, and until now it was the only one nobody had told.

      `Global\DevOnBike.Overfit.MachineMeasurement` is taken by the benchmark host (Sources/Benchmark/
      Program.cs) and by the test suite (Tests/MeasurementExclusion.cs), so benchmark-vs-benchmark,
      benchmark-vs-tests and tests-vs-tests are all guarded. Build-vs-anything was not, and on 2026-08-13 a
      `dotnet build` ran inside a live [LongFact] window, rewriting assemblies a test host had already
      loaded. It is the prime suspect for the stall that followed (docs/TASKS.md, XC-42 and PB-7).

      CHECK, NOT LOCK, and the distinction is the whole design. This target probes the mutex and fails; it
      never takes it. `dotnet test` and `dotnet run` against Sources/Benchmark both BUILD FIRST, so a build
      that held the lock would deadlock the very commands it exists to protect. Probing covers the direction
      that caused harm — a human building while something measures — and leaves the reverse to the mutex the
      measuring processes already share.

      IT FAILS OPEN. Anything the probe cannot determine — a permissions error, an unreadable handle, a
      platform that does not name mutexes the same way — lets the build through. A wrongly blocked build
      costs more than one contaminated sample, and a guard that stops the whole repository compiling is
      ripped out within a week rather than fixed.

      ONCE PER BUILD, not once per project: gated on Main, the same way the prerelease check above is,
      because this solution has 26 projects and a message printed 26 times is wallpaper. The consequence,
      stated rather than discovered: building a project that does not reference Main is unguarded.

      THREE WAYS PAST IT, each deliberate.
        OVERFIT_ALLOW_CONCURRENT_MEASUREMENT=1  the same named escape hatch the test suite already reads.
        OVERFIT_MEASUREMENT_OWNER=<pid>         set by the benchmark host before it starts BenchmarkDotNet.
                                                BDN compiles a generated project per job WHILE the run holds
                                                the mutex, and child processes inherit the environment, so
                                                this is what stops the guard deadlocking a benchmark against
                                                itself. Ownership rather than exemption: the build belongs to
                                                the run in progress.
        -p:OverfitBuildExclusionCheck=false     one build, no environment change.

      Inline task rather than an analyzer, which is against this repository's direction of travel and is a
      deliberate exception: MSBuild property functions cannot call Mutex.TryOpenExisting (it is not on the
      allow-list), and an analyzer runs inside the compiler, which is already too late to stop a build from
      writing over a loaded assembly. The body is ten lines with no parsing in it — unlike the 120 lines of
      regex that were moved out of Main.csproj in 2026-08-05, which is what that comment is about.
    -->
    <UsingTask TaskName="OverfitMeasurementProbe"
               TaskFactory="RoslynCodeTaskFactory"
               AssemblyFile="$(MSBuildToolsPath)\Microsoft.Build.Tasks.Core.dll">
        <ParameterGroup>
            <MutexName ParameterType="System.String" Required="true" />
            <Held ParameterType="System.Boolean" Output="true" />
        </ParameterGroup>
        <Task>
            <Using Namespace="System" />
            <Using Namespace="System.Threading" />
            <Code Type="Fragment" Language="cs"><![CDATA[
                Held = false;

                try
                {
                    Mutex existing;

                    // Existence is the signal, not ownership: both participants create the mutex and
                    // acquire it immediately, and hold the handle for the whole run. A handle that exists
                    // therefore means a run is in progress.
                    if (Mutex.TryOpenExisting(MutexName, out existing))
                    {
                        using (existing)
                        {
                            Held = true;
                        }
                    }
                }
                catch (Exception)
                {
                    // Fail OPEN, deliberately. A permissions error on a Global\ handle would otherwise
                    // block every build on this machine for a reason nobody could see.
                    Held = false;
                }
            ]]></Code>
        </Task>
    </UsingTask>

    <Target Name="OverfitBuildExclusionCheck"
            BeforeTargets="CoreCompile"
            Condition="'$(OverfitBuildExclusionCheck)' != 'false'
                       AND '$(MSBuildProjectName)' == 'Main'
                       AND '$(OVERFIT_ALLOW_CONCURRENT_MEASUREMENT)' != '1'
                       AND '$(OVERFIT_MEASUREMENT_OWNER)' == ''">

        <OverfitMeasurementProbe MutexName="Global\DevOnBike.Overfit.MachineMeasurement">
            <Output TaskParameter="Held" PropertyName="_OverfitMeasurementHeld" />
        </OverfitMeasurementProbe>

        <Error Condition="'$(_OverfitMeasurementHeld)' == 'true'"
               Code="OVERFITMEASURING"
               File="$(MSBuildThisFileDirectory)Directory.Build.targets"
               Text="A benchmark or a test run holds Global\DevOnBike.Overfit.MachineMeasurement, so this build is refusing to start: it would rewrite assemblies a live test host has already loaded (that cost an hour on 2026-08-13) and land a compile inside somebody's sampling window. SOMETHING ALIVE holds it - a holder that died raises AbandonedMutexException, which both participants treat as free.%0AFind it by NAME PATTERN, never by process class; a healthy run is a matched pair (DevOnBike.Overfit.Tests.exe beside a testhost.exe), and the test executable alive with NO testhost beside it is an orphan. Kill the process TREE, not the runner.%0A  Get-CimInstance Win32_Process | Where-Object { $_.Name -match 'Overfit|Tests' } | Select-Object ProcessId,ParentProcessId,Name,CreationDate,CommandLine%0AWait for the run to finish, or set OVERFIT_ALLOW_CONCURRENT_MEASUREMENT=1 if you know they are not sharing a box." />

    </Target>

</Project>
